# PART 3 — TUMAKURU GEOGRAPHICAL OPERATING CONTEXT

## 9. CANONICAL CITY CONTEXT

The initial operating area for UrbanPulse is **Tumakuru, Karnataka, India**. The name **Tumkur** may appear in older map data, legacy records, spelling variations, or user-entered text. Treat `Tumakuru` as the preferred display spelling while allowing `Tumkur` as a recognized search alias where a geocoder or data source supports it.

Use a clear canonical place context such as:

`Tumakuru, Karnataka, India`

Do not mistake Tumakuru city for the entire Tumakuru district. Do not automatically expand the operational map to the whole district merely because the city name is also the district name.

## 10. CITY CENTER AND INITIAL CAMERA POSITION

The initial map view should open to the configured Tumakuru urban operating area when no specific report, user location, or saved viewport is available.

Resolve the initial city camera using a verified geocoding result or trusted configured coordinates. Do not invent a precise latitude/longitude pair and label it as an authoritative city center.

The default camera should:

- Show the configured Tumakuru urban extent at a useful city-level zoom.
- Keep the main operating area visible.
- Avoid zooming so far out that the city becomes a tiny point in Karnataka.
- Avoid zooming so far in that only one street is visible.
- Use the stored user viewport when the user is returning to a session, if the application already supports this safely.
- Refit to the selected report or heatmap cell when the user opens its details.

## 11. CITY BOUNDARY AND JURISDICTION RULES

Use an authoritative or explicitly approved geographical boundary dataset for Tumakuru's operating jurisdiction wherever one is available.

Acceptable provenance may include:

- An official municipal/corporation/city authority dataset.
- An official ward boundary dataset.
- A government GIS dataset with known source and date.
- A validated administrative-boundary dataset approved for this project.
- A carefully documented open geographical dataset used as a non-authoritative map context where official data is unavailable.

Do not fabricate:

- Ward polygons.
- Ward names or ward numbers.
- City limits.
- Municipal jurisdiction.
- Road hierarchy.
- Neighbourhood boundaries.
- Facility locations.
- Water-body boundaries.
- Public-service locations.

If the official city boundary or ward dataset is unavailable, do not pretend that a guessed polygon is official. Use the geocoded city viewport for initial camera framing only, and mark jurisdiction/boundary validation as unavailable.

A map viewport is not an administrative boundary. A geocoder's bounding box is not automatically an official municipal limit. A road basemap is not proof of municipal ownership.

## 12. REPORTS OUTSIDE THE OPERATING BOUNDARY

If a report coordinate falls outside the configured Tumakuru operating boundary:

- Do not silently move it into the city.
- Do not clamp it to the nearest city edge.
- Do not change the coordinate to make the map look tidy.
- Do not automatically delete it.
- Mark the jurisdiction status as `OUTSIDE_CONFIGURED_AREA` only when a valid boundary polygon exists and the coordinate can be evaluated against it.
- If the boundary is missing or uncertain, use `JURISDICTION_UNVERIFIED`.
- Preserve the report and its source coordinates.
- Allow an authorized reviewer to decide whether it should be retained, reassigned, or rejected under the platform's operating policy.

The map may provide a filter for all reports, in-area reports, out-of-area reports, and unverified-jurisdiction reports, but the source record must remain unchanged.

## 13. WARD-LEVEL ANALYSIS

If validated Tumakuru ward boundaries become available, support ward-level spatial aggregation.

For each issue, determine whether the issue coordinate falls inside a ward polygon using a correct point-in-polygon operation and the official boundary version.

Store or derive:

- Ward ID.
- Ward name/number, if supplied by the boundary source.
- Boundary dataset name.
- Boundary dataset version/date.
- Spatial match status.
- Whether the point is near a boundary.
- Whether GPS uncertainty overlaps multiple wards.

If the GPS uncertainty circle overlaps more than one ward, do not force a single ward assignment without a stated rule. Return `WARD_AMBIGUOUS` or use a clearly marked approximate assignment.

Never invent ward data to make a ward filter look complete.

