# PART 4 — THE TWO REQUIRED MAP MODES

## 14. MODE A — PINPOINT REPORT MAP

Create a map mode named clearly, such as `Pinpoint Map`, `Report Pins`, or `Individual Issues`.

Its purpose is to show where individual reported/identified civic issues are located, subject to the accuracy of their location data.

The pinpoint map must:

- Display each independently located physical issue as a discrete point feature.
- Display a report-observation point when only report-level GPS exists and individual object coordinates do not.
- Distinguish exact/verified issue coordinates from approximate/report-capture coordinates.
- Use severity-based marker colours according to the shared legend in this prompt.
- Show a marker cluster when multiple nearby features overlap at lower zoom levels.
- Allow users to zoom into a cluster and inspect the individual locations.
- Allow a marker to be selected to reveal its issue/report information.
- Show the linked AI category, severity, priority, verification state, report count, unresolved duration, and location accuracy where available.
- Provide a direct relationship back to the original report and AI analysis.
- Never create an issue record merely because a marker was rendered.

### 14.1 One point must mean one clearly defined thing

Every point feature must declare its feature type. Use a controlled value such as:

- `PHYSICAL_ISSUE_POINT`
- `REPORT_OBSERVATION_POINT`
- `APPROXIMATE_LOCATION_POINT`
- `CLUSTER_FEATURE`

A `PHYSICAL_ISSUE_POINT` means the coordinates refer to a particular physical issue with a defensible location association.

A `REPORT_OBSERVATION_POINT` means the coordinates represent where the report was captured, submitted, or manually located. It does not claim that every object detected in the image is exactly at that coordinate.

An `APPROXIMATE_LOCATION_POINT` means a usable approximate place is available but its precision is insufficient for exact pin interpretation.

A `CLUSTER_FEATURE` is a visual grouping of several points at the current zoom. It is not a physical issue and must not inflate report totals.

### 14.2 Marker colour must have one stable meaning

Use the severity-colour mapping specified in Part 15. Do not change the colour meaning between zoom levels, map modes, or category filters.

### 14.3 Marker identity and tooltip/detail data

A marker's underlying identity must remain stable when the dataset refreshes. Use the physical issue identifier where available; otherwise use a stable report/location-feature identifier derived from persistent source identifiers, not a random value created on every render.

Marker details should include only data that actually exists:

- Issue/report reference.
- Category.
- Visible problem summary.
- AI severity score and label.
- AI priority score and label.
- Current issue status.
- Verification state.
- Report count and confirmed independent report count, if supplied.
- First report date and latest update date, if valid.
- GPS source and accuracy.
- Location precision status.
- Linked report references.

Never expose reporter names, phone numbers, email addresses, account IDs, or private personal metadata on public map markers.

## 15. SHARED SEVERITY COLOUR SYSTEM

The same severity colour meaning must be used in both the pinpoint map and the severity heatmap.

The default UrbanPulse severity palette is deliberately simple and consistent:

| AI severity tier | AI severity score | Map colour | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|---|
| LOW | 0–24 | GREEN | Simple/low-severity condition |
| MODERATE | 25–49 | BLUE | Moderate visible problem |
| HIGH | 50–74 | RED | Serious/high-severity problem |
| CRITICAL | 75–100 | RED, with a stronger visual emphasis | Critical visible problem |
| UNKNOWN / NOT ASSESSED | null or unavailable | NEUTRAL GREY | Severity is unknown; do not imply that the issue is normal |

The score ranges follow the severity scale defined in the separate UrbanPulse AI master prompt. This map prompt must not silently change the thresholds.

Use red for high and critical conditions, blue for moderate conditions, and green for low/simple conditions. Use neutral grey for unknown or unassessed severity.

### 15.1 Important distinction: green does not mean fixed

Green means low visual severity according to the AI assessment. It does not mean resolved, safe, approved, or repaired.

A resolved issue must be identified by its status, not by changing its severity colour to green.

A low-severity unresolved problem remains green in the severity-colour scheme unless its AI severity assessment changes through a new valid assessment or approved human review.

### 15.2 Important distinction: colour is severity, not report volume

A red marker means the associated issue has a high/critical AI severity tier. It does not mean that many people reported it.

A blue marker means moderate severity. It does not mean that the issue is in a central area.

A green marker means low severity. It does not mean that the issue has been resolved.

Report count, priority, verification, age, and category must be displayed as separate fields or visual signals. Do not overload the colour with multiple meanings.

### 15.3 Accessibility and contrast

The colours must remain distinguishable against the map basemap in both light and dark themes if the application supports both.

Do not rely on colour alone. Include a text label, legend, accessible description, or severity badge. Consider users with colour-vision deficiencies. Use clear contrast and avoid extremely transparent markers that make the colour unreadable.

Do not use a gradient in which blue or green can be mistaken for a category label. The legend must always explain the current colour mapping.

## 16. MODE B — SEVERITY HEATMAP

Create a second map mode named clearly, such as `Severity Heatmap` or `Problem Hotspots`.

Its purpose is to show the geographical distribution and concentration of issues at an area level rather than to show every individual marker independently.

The heatmap must:

- Aggregate actual eligible physical issues by geographical area/cell.
- Avoid counting duplicate reports as separate physical issues when they have been confirmed as duplicates of the same issue.
- Avoid treating several AI detections in one report as several independent citizen reports.
- Preserve separate category, severity, priority, verification, and status counts.
- Show a geographical concentration pattern that can be explained and reproduced.
- Use the shared green/blue/red severity palette.
- Make the aggregation cell size or spatial resolution appropriate to the current zoom and configured policy.
- Let the user inspect a cell to understand which underlying issues contributed to it.
- Respect the same report filters used by the pinpoint map.
- Recalculate or refresh when relevant source reports or AI assessments change.

A heatmap is a summary. It must not pretend to locate each underlying issue precisely when the source coordinates are approximate.

## 17. TWO-MODE SWITCHING CONTRACT

The user must be able to switch between `PINPOINT_REPORT_MAP` and `SEVERITY_HEATMAP` using a clear mode control.

Switching modes must change the map representation, not the dataset identity or filter logic.

Preserve between modes:

- Selected categories.
- Selected severity tiers.
- Priority filters.
- Verification filters.
- Issue-status filters.
- Date/time range.
- Ward/area filters, where valid.
- Search term, if applicable.
- Current map bounds/viewport where sensible.
- Selected issue or selected cell context where it can be preserved meaningfully.
- The currently selected operating area.

Do not reset all filters every time the user switches modes.

Do not silently change the time range when the mode changes.

Do not show one set of reports in pinpoint mode and a different, unexplained set in heatmap mode.

Both modes must use the same eligibility rules and the same canonical dataset snapshot for a given filter state.

If the selected issue exists in the pinpoint map but the user switches to the heatmap, highlight or identify its containing cell where possible. If the selected cell is opened in pinpoint mode, filter or highlight its contributing issue points where supported.

