# PART 12 — HEATMAP INTERACTIONS

## 66. CELL SELECTION

Selecting a heatmap cell should reveal the aggregate summary and the issue list that produced it.

The user must be able to move from area-level understanding to individual issue details.

Provide a route from:

`Heatmap Cell → Contributing Issue → Original Report → AI Assessment`

The transition must preserve the source identity and must not create a new report.

## 67. CELL DRILL-DOWN

When a cell is selected:

- Highlight the selected cell.
- Show its unique issue count.
- Show category and severity distribution.
- Show verified/unverified status counts.
- Show unresolved count.
- Show source data time range.
- Show the cell resolution.
- Show whether any contributing location is approximate.
- Provide access to the underlying issue list.
- Allow a user to switch to pinpoint mode focused on the cell's contributing issues.

If the cell contains many issues, use pagination or a bounded list rather than loading an unlimited amount of detail at once.

## 68. CELL COLOUR RE-EVALUATION

If a contributing issue's severity changes after new AI analysis or human review, update the cell's severity counts and colour.

If a high/critical issue is resolved and the default filter excludes resolved issues, the cell may change colour because the eligible set changed. The system must not erase its historical record.

If the heatmap shows all statuses, the resolved issue should remain represented with an explicit status distinction rather than silently disappearing.

## 69. CELL BOUNDARY MEANING

A grid/hexagon cell is an analytical aggregation area. It is not necessarily a neighbourhood, ward, street, or official municipal zone.

Do not label a heatmap cell with a ward name unless it has been spatially matched to a validated ward boundary.

Do not imply that the cell centre is the exact location of any report.

---

# PART 13 — STATUS, VERIFICATION, AND TIME-BASED MAP BEHAVIOUR

## 70. ACTIVE VS. RESOLVED ISSUES

Define the default map eligibility policy from the application's actual status model.

A useful default is to focus the initial civic-priority map on active/unresolved issues, while allowing users to include resolved and historical issues through filters.

Do not assume that every record is open.

Do not infer resolution from the absence of a new report.

Do not infer that a problem has been fixed because its marker disappeared from a filtered view.

Keep resolved, rejected, duplicate, reopened, pending, and active states distinct wherever the application supports them.

## 71. VERIFICATION STATE IS NOT LOCATION ACCURACY

An AI-detected issue may be visually well supported but geographically approximate.

A report may have precise GPS but remain unverified as a civic problem.

A human-verified issue may still have a low-precision location.

Represent these independently.

Do not use a precise GPS coordinate as proof that the pothole or garbage report itself is genuine.

Do not use a high AI detection confidence as proof that the GPS is accurate.

## 72. TIME FILTERS AND HISTORICAL MAPS

Support appropriate time-based filtering using valid report timestamps.

Potential views include:

- Current active issue map.
- New reports within a selected period.
- All reports within a selected period.
- Historical unresolved issue map.
- Resolved issues within a selected period.
- Issue creation trend by geography.

For time-based heatmaps, explicitly identify which event time is used: first reported time, report submission time, image capture time, resolution time, or latest update time.

Do not compare different time definitions without labelling them.

## 73. HISTORICAL SNAPSHOTS

If the system supports historical heatmap snapshots, ensure that each snapshot records:

- Snapshot creation time.
- Data time window.
- Filter configuration.
- Severity scale version.
- Spatial aggregation method.
- Cell resolution.
- Eligible issue IDs or a reproducible source version.
- Data-source version/refresh time.

Do not present a historical snapshot as live data.

Do not recompute an old snapshot using current issue status and then label it as the map state from the past.

---

# PART 14 — LIVE DATA, REFRESH, AND CONSISTENCY

## 74. MAP DATA MUST FOLLOW REPORT LIFECYCLE EVENTS

The maps should reflect relevant report lifecycle updates from the existing application.

Relevant events may include:

- Report created.
- Report image uploaded.
- AI analysis started.
- AI analysis completed.
- AI analysis failed or partially completed.
- Detection added or corrected.
- Physical issue linked to a report.
- Duplicate relationship confirmed or rejected.
- Location captured.
- Location corrected.
- Location verified.
- Severity reassessed.
- Priority reassessed.
- Verification status changed.
- Issue status changed.
- Issue resolved.
- Issue reopened.

Use the existing event/subscription/polling mechanism where possible.

## 75. NEW REPORT BEFORE AI ANALYSIS FINISHES

A report may exist before AI analysis has completed.

When a valid location exists but AI severity is pending:

- It may appear as an unassessed/neutral map feature if the product policy allows pending reports to be shown.
- Do not assign green/blue/red without an actual severity value.
- Mark its AI assessment as pending.
- Do not invent a provisional class or severity.

When AI analysis completes, update the feature from the authoritative assessment.

If analysis fails, show the appropriate pending/failed state and preserve the report location if valid.

## 76. LIVE UPDATE CONSISTENCY

When the same report changes, both map modes must update from the same new source state.

Avoid temporary situations where:

- Pinpoint mode shows a report as resolved but heatmap mode still counts it as open.
- Pinpoint mode uses new severity while heatmap mode uses an older severity.
- A location correction moves a marker but leaves the heatmap cell in its old position.
- A duplicate merge changes the issue count but not the heatmap count.
- A report is deleted/withdrawn but remains as a stale marker.

Use source versioning, timestamps, cache invalidation, or a similar consistency mechanism appropriate to the current architecture.

## 77. REFRESH POLICY

Refresh only the data that needs refreshing where possible, but prioritize correctness over premature optimization.

A refresh must not:

- Duplicate markers.
- Duplicate heatmap contributions.
- Reset the camera unexpectedly.
- Clear filters.
- Lose the selected issue unnecessarily.
- Create a second physical issue identity.

If the data source is temporarily unavailable, show the last successful refresh time and clearly label stale data. Do not present stale data as live.

---

# PART 15 — SPATIAL DATA PERFORMANCE AND SCALABILITY

## 78. VIEWPORT-AWARE LOADING

Do not load every historical report and every image into the browser merely to render a city map.

Where supported by the existing architecture, retrieve the data needed for the current viewport, filter state, and zoom level.

Use pagination, bounding-box queries, server-side aggregation, spatial indexes, or another appropriate strategy for the existing data platform.

Do not fetch full image binaries merely to draw markers.

Do not send the entire report history to the browser if only map feature fields are needed.

## 79. SPATIAL INDEXING

If the underlying database supports spatial indexes, use them appropriately for location queries and viewport filtering.

If it does not, use the safest scalable query pattern compatible with the current system and document any limits.

Do not introduce a complex GIS database without a clear need and a safe migration plan.

## 80. HEATMAP AGGREGATION LOCATION

For a small dataset, client-side aggregation may be acceptable if it is deterministic, efficient, and uses the same filtered source data.

As report volume increases, consider server-side or precomputed aggregation so the client does not need to load every point.

Whichever approach is selected, the aggregation logic must be:

- Reproducible.
- Versioned.
- Filter-aware.
- Based on unique physical issues.
- Consistent between users viewing the same data snapshot.
- Auditable back to source issue records.

Do not maintain separate competing versions of the severity aggregation formula in the browser and server.

## 81. PERFORMANCE RULES

Keep the map responsive when the data includes many reports.

Use techniques appropriate to the chosen library, such as:

- Marker clustering.
- Viewport filtering.
- GeoJSON source updates rather than recreating the entire map.
- Canvas/WebGL rendering for large point sets where appropriate.
- Server-side spatial aggregation.
- Debounced filter requests.
- Cached geospatial results with safe invalidation.
- Lazy-loaded marker detail.
- Limited visible detail at low zoom.
- Appropriate cell resolution for the current zoom.

Do not sacrifice data correctness in order to make an animation appear smooth.

## 82. CACHING AND INVALIDATION

Cache only derived map data whose source version and filter inputs are known.

A cache key should account for relevant values such as:

- Data-source version/updated-at timestamp.
- Current status filter.
- Category filter.
- Severity filter.
- Priority filter.
- Date range.
- Jurisdiction/ward selection.
- Spatial viewport or cell resolution.
- Aggregation algorithm version.
- Severity rubric version where necessary.

Invalidate affected map aggregates when source reports, locations, severity scores, physical issue associations, status, or filters change.

Do not use a stale heatmap cache after a location has moved to a different cell.

---

# PART 16 — MAP PROVIDER, BASEMAP, AND GEODATA RULES

## 83. BASEMAP PROVIDER SELECTION

Use one suitable map provider/renderer consistent with the existing application.

Possible provider families include commercial map APIs and open-source map-rendering ecosystems. Select only after checking current project dependencies, provider capability, API terms, pricing, and existing deployment configuration.

Do not include multiple base maps merely for decoration.

## 84. CURRENT API SUPPORT CHECK

Before implementing heatmap rendering, check the currently supported API and library versions.

Do not copy an old tutorial that uses an API already deprecated or decommissioned.

For example, the Google Maps JavaScript API `HeatmapLayer` has been decommissioned; if the application uses Google Maps, select a current supported third-party heatmap integration or another supported rendering approach rather than relying on that removed feature.

Treat this as a version-sensitive requirement and re-check official provider documentation at implementation time.

## 85. OPEN MAP DATA AND TILE USAGE

If OpenStreetMap-derived data or tiles are used:

- Follow the relevant tile provider's usage policy.
- Display the required attribution visibly.
- Use an approved tile service or self-hosted arrangement appropriate to actual traffic.
- Respect caching rules and service limits.
- Do not bulk-download or scrape public tile servers.
- Do not assume that free map data means unlimited free tile infrastructure.
- Do not use a public tile endpoint as an undocumented production guarantee.
- Record the tile/provider source and its attribution requirement.

Open map data and a particular tile server are different things. Respect both the data licence and the tile-service policy.

## 86. API KEYS AND SECRETS

Never hard-code private map-provider API keys or geocoding secrets in source files committed to a repository.

Use the application's established environment configuration.

Apply domain restrictions, API restrictions, quota limits, and provider security guidance where supported.

Do not expose privileged server-side geocoding credentials in browser code.

Do not write a fake API key or claim that a map service is configured when it is not.

If a required key is unavailable:

- Show a clear configuration error.
- Keep the rest of the app usable where possible.
- Do not switch silently to an unrelated provider that violates usage terms.
- Document exactly which environment variable or provider configuration is missing without exposing secret values.

## 87. GEOCODING AND REVERSE GEOCODING

Use a trusted geocoding provider for converting a typed place/address to coordinates or coordinates to a human-readable label.

Geocoding results are contextual labels, not proof that the underlying report location is exact.

Store:

- Provider name.
- Provider result/reference ID where available.
- Returned label.
- Result type or precision if provided.
- Query time.
- Coordinates returned.
- Any result confidence/quality indicator the provider actually supplies.

Do not invent a road name from coordinates.

Do not treat a city-level geocode result as a street-level position.

Do not reverse-geocode every incoming GPS point without a valid need; respect rate limits, caching, privacy, and provider terms.

## 88. GEOGRAPHIC DATA PROVENANCE

Any data used to determine city boundaries, wards, roads, facilities, or public-space categories must have a recorded source and version/date where possible.

Do not display a school, hospital, bus stop, road, ward, or municipal facility as verified merely because a general search result suggested it exists nearby.

Do not invent a local landmark list for Tumakuru.

If a facility layer is unavailable, omit it or mark it unavailable. Do not fabricate one to make geographical importance appear more sophisticated.

---

# PART 17 — PRIVACY, CONSENT, AND LOCATION SECURITY

## 89. MINIMIZE LOCATION COLLECTION

Collect only the location information needed to locate the reported civic problem.

Do not collect or store continuous movement history by default.

Do not record a reporter's route through Tumakuru.

Do not retain repeated background GPS samples when one location observation is sufficient.

Do not request GPS permission merely to view the public map.

## 90. ISSUE LOCATION VS. REPORTER LOCATION

The map is about civic issues, not the private whereabouts of the reporter.

Store and expose the issue/report observation location as needed for the civic purpose.

Do not show the reporter's current location to other users.

Do not show reporter identity or contact details in public map summaries.

Do not create public links that expose private user-location history.

## 91. IMAGE METADATA PRIVACY

Images may contain EXIF GPS and device metadata.

Before using embedded metadata:

- Confirm the application policy permits it.
- Inform the user where appropriate.
- Validate its timestamp and coordinate format.
- Do not assume EXIF GPS is present or trustworthy.
- Do not expose private camera/device metadata in public map details.
- Do not copy unnecessary EXIF fields into public report records.

## 92. ACCESS CONTROL

Public users should see only the location/report information that the application's access policy allows.

Authorized reviewers may see additional validation details if permitted.

Never bypass the existing authorization model to make map details load more easily.

Do not expose internal reviewer notes or private location corrections to public users unless the application explicitly permits them.

## 93. LOCATION SPOOFING AND ABUSE

Treat GPS spoofing, impossible jumps, malformed coordinates, repeated suspicious submissions, and coordinate poisoning as potential data-quality concerns.

Do not automatically accuse a user of fraud based on a single anomaly.

Use validation flags and human review.

Check:

- Coordinate ranges.
- Location timestamps.
- Speed/distance plausibility if multiple trusted samples exist and the workflow actually needs that check.
- Repeated identical coordinates across unrelated reports.
- Suspiciously reused image metadata.
- Unexpected coordinate changes after submission.
- Inconsistent manual and device locations.

Do not reveal detection heuristics in a way that unnecessarily helps someone bypass them.

---

# PART 18 — ERROR HANDLING AND FAILURE STATES

## 94. GPS FAILURE

If GPS fails, the report remains valid as a report if the existing report policy permits it.

Mark location unavailable and provide manual location selection where supported.

Do not invent coordinates.

## 95. MAP TILE FAILURE

If base-map tiles fail:

- Show a clear map-service error or fallback state.
- Preserve the report data and filters.
- Avoid showing markers floating on a blank background without context unless the user can still understand the coordinate grid.
- Do not delete or mutate source data.
- Retry according to a reasonable network policy.

## 96. GEOCODING FAILURE

If reverse geocoding fails:

- Preserve latitude/longitude.
- Display coordinates or an unavailable label if appropriate.
- Do not fabricate an address.
- Do not block the map point if the coordinate itself is valid.

## 97. HEATMAP FAILURE

If heatmap aggregation or rendering fails:

- Keep the pinpoint report map usable.
- Show an explicit heatmap error state.
- Do not display a stale heatmap as if it were current.
- Provide a retry path.
- Record the failure context for diagnostics without exposing secrets.

## 98. PARTIAL DATA FAILURE

If some records are malformed but others are valid:

- Render valid records.
- Exclude invalid coordinates from spatial calculations.
- Keep invalid/unlocated records in the non-mappable report count.
- Report the number excluded and why, where authorized.
- Do not let one malformed report crash the entire map.

## 99. EMPTY DATA

If there are no eligible reports, show a real empty state.

Do not display sample pins, synthetic hotspots, or random demo data in the production view.

Explain whether the area has no reports, the active filters exclude all reports, or the data has not loaded.

These are different situations and should not be confused.

## 100. NETWORK LOSS AND STALE DATA

When offline or disconnected:

- Preserve the last usable map state if appropriate.
- Clearly mark data as stale/offline.
- Do not imply new reports have been received.
- Do not claim the heatmap reflects the current state.
- Reconcile refreshed data safely when the connection returns.

---

# PART 19 — MAP QUALITY, CONSISTENCY, AND ANTI-MISREPRESENTATION RULES

## 101. NO RANDOM MAP DATA

The following are forbidden in the real report view:

- Randomly generated report coordinates.
- Hard-coded fake pothole locations.
- Decorative sample heat spots.
- Fake report counts.
- Fake severity values.
- Fake ward boundaries.
- Invented addresses.
- Invented road names.
- Invented public facilities.
- Fake real-time updates.
- Fake GPS accuracy.

Development fixtures must be separate, labelled, and excluded from production configuration.

## 102. NO AUTOMATIC COORDINATE CORRECTION WITHOUT EVIDENCE

Do not shift a coordinate simply because it looks wrong on the map.

Do not force a point onto the nearest road.

Do not move an out-of-area point to the nearest in-area location.

Do not change a coordinate to fit a heatmap cell.

All corrections must be explicit, sourced, and auditable.

## 103. NO MISLEADING HEATMAP

Do not:

- Colour empty cells green.
- Colour unknown severity green.
- Treat report count as severity.
- Treat multiple images as multiple issues.
- Treat several detections within one image as independent citizen reports.
- Treat a marker cluster as a duplicate issue.
- Hide a severe issue by averaging it with many low-severity issues.
- Label a cell as a ward without verified ward data.
- Present a cell centre as an exact report location.
- Use an unlabelled blur radius that makes distant issues appear adjacent.

## 104. NO CONTRADICTORY STATUS VISUALS

A red marker may be resolved; a green marker may be unresolved. These are not contradictory because severity and status are separate dimensions.

Do not change severity colour when status changes unless the underlying severity assessment itself changes.

Use status as a separate label, symbol, outline, or text value.

## 105. NO FALSE PRECISION

If the location is approximate, use an approximate-location style and show the uncertainty.

If the location is report-level rather than object-level, label it as such.

If a point is only known to a neighbourhood, do not draw a street-level pin.

If the GPS accuracy is 80 metres, do not imply a 1-metre position.

If a ward match is ambiguous, show ambiguity.

If a heatmap cell contains only two reports, do not describe it as a statistically proven hotspot.

---

# PART 20 — SECURITY AND DATA VALIDATION

## 106. INPUT VALIDATION

Validate every location record before it reaches map calculations.

Validate:

- Coordinate type.
- Coordinate range.
- Coordinate finiteness.
- Accuracy value type and non-negative range.
- Timestamp format.
- Timestamp chronology.
- Source enum.
- Location type enum.
- Validation state enum.
- Physical issue/report identifiers.
- Severity score range.
- Priority score range.
- Category enum.
- Status enum.

Reject or quarantine malformed geospatial fields without deleting the whole report unless the application's integrity policy requires it.

## 107. OUTPUT ENCODING AND UNTRUSTED TEXT

Treat report descriptions, place labels, geocoder results, and external map data as untrusted input.

Escape text appropriately before display.

Do not execute report text as code or interpret geocoder strings as instructions.

Do not let a malicious report description alter map filter logic, query structure, or AI analysis configuration.

## 108. API AND QUERY SAFETY

Use parameterized or safely constructed queries for location filters and report retrieval.

Do not concatenate untrusted coordinates, labels, IDs, or search text into executable queries.

Enforce authorization on report and location endpoints.

Do not trust client-provided `is_verified`, `is_authorized`, `physical_issue_id`, or priority fields without checking the authoritative data source.

## 109. PUBLIC DATA MINIMIZATION

Return to the public map only the fields required for map operation and allowed by the access policy.

Do not send large private report objects to the client just to render one marker.

Do not include personal contact details in heatmap aggregation or marker metadata.

---

# PART 21 — MAP ANALYTICS AND AREA INTELLIGENCE

## 110. AREA SUMMARY

Where the application has adequate data, provide an area summary that can report:

- Unique physical issues in the selected area.
- Report submissions in the selected area.
- Pothole count.
- Garbage/waste count.
- Other civic candidate count.
- Low/moderate/high/critical severity counts.
- Unknown-severity count.
- Unresolved issue count.
- Verified issue count.
- Unverified issue count.
- Confirmed independent reports.
- Average severity, if statistically meaningful.
- Highest severity.
- Average priority, if available.
- Oldest unresolved issue, if valid.
- Unlocated issue count, if relevant to the selected area cannot be known then do not attribute unlocated issues to it.

All totals must be based on the same filter state and the same underlying data snapshot.

## 111. AREA COMPARISON

If comparing neighbourhoods or wards, only use verified geographical units.

Use comparable date windows and comparable filters.

Normalize counts by area or population only when the required area/population data is available and reliable.

Do not compare raw counts from a large ward to a small ward as if their sizes were equal.

Do not infer population from the number of reports.

Do not claim that a high-reporting area is necessarily the most dangerous area. Reporting behaviour, awareness, smartphone access, and data completeness can influence counts.

## 112. HOTSPOT LANGUAGE

Use cautious language such as:

- `Higher concentration of reported issues`
- `Area containing high-severity reports`
- `Potential civic-problem hotspot`
- `More reports in the selected period`

Avoid unsupported language such as:

- `Most dangerous area`
- `Worst road in Tumakuru`
- `Accident-prone zone`
- `Confirmed unsafe neighbourhood`

unless independent, valid evidence supports those claims and the system is authorized to make them.

## 113. REPORTING BIAS AND COVERAGE

A heatmap shows the reports available to UrbanPulse, not necessarily every problem that exists in the city.

Do not claim that a green area has no civic problems. It may have no reports or poor reporting coverage.

Where feasible, distinguish:

- No reported issues.
- No eligible issues after filters.
- No mapped issues because coordinates are unavailable.
- Low report coverage.
- Data unavailable.

A lack of reports is not proof of a lack of problems.

---

# PART 22 — TESTING AND VALIDATION REQUIREMENTS

## 114. TEST THE WHOLE REPORT-TO-MAP PATH

Do not test only whether a base map appears.

Test the complete relationship:

`Citizen Report → Location Capture → Report Persistence → AI Analysis → Issue/Report Association → Map Adapter → Pinpoint Map → Heatmap Aggregation → Filtered Detail`

The test must prove that a real report can travel through the full pipeline and appear in both map modes with the correct identity, coordinates, severity, status, and provenance.

## 115. GPS TEST CASES

Test at least the following:

1. GPS permission granted with high precision.
2. GPS permission granted with moderate precision.
3. GPS permission denied.
4. GPS permission blocked by browser settings.
5. Device location services disabled.
6. Location request timeout.
7. Browser does not support geolocation.
8. Secure context requirement not met.
9. GPS returns invalid latitude.
10. GPS returns invalid longitude.
11. Latitude and longitude accidentally swapped.
12. GPS accuracy is missing.
13. GPS accuracy is negative or malformed.
14. Capture timestamp missing.
15. GPS timestamp older than image timestamp.
16. Upload occurs later from a different location.
17. User manually selects a location.
18. User corrects the map pin.
19. Reviewer verifies a corrected location.
20. Original location remains auditable after correction.
21. Report has no usable location.
22. Report is outside Tumakuru operating boundary.
23. Jurisdiction boundary is unavailable.
24. GPS uncertainty overlaps two ward boundaries.
25. Several reports have identical coordinates.
26. Same issue has slightly different GPS coordinates across reports.
27. One report contains multiple detections but only one GPS point.
28. Dashcam video contains multiple GPS observations.
29. Location source conflicts with image/report context.
30. Device reports a stale cached position.

## 116. PINPOINT MAP TEST CASES

Test at least:

1. One valid pothole issue appears as one point.
2. One valid garbage issue appears as one point.
3. One issue has multiple reports but appears once at issue level.
4. Two distinct potholes near each other remain separate issues.
5. A cluster expands to its underlying points.
6. Two overlapping markers remain inspectable.
7. Unknown severity uses neutral styling.
8. Low severity uses green.
9. Moderate severity uses blue.
10. High severity uses red.
11. Critical severity uses red with stronger emphasis.
12. Status changes without altering severity colour.
13. Selection does not alter source data.
14. A location correction moves the point after persistence.
15. A report without coordinates is not falsely mapped.
16. A report-observation point is not described as an exact object location.
17. A marker opens the correct source report.
18. Marker details show the linked AI assessment.
19. Private reporter data is not exposed.
20. Out-of-area reports are represented according to jurisdiction policy.

## 117. HEATMAP TEST CASES

Test at least:

1. One low-severity issue creates a green cell.
2. One moderate-severity issue creates a blue cell.
3. One high-severity issue creates a red cell.
4. One critical issue creates a red cell.
5. Unknown severity does not create a green cell.
6. A cell with multiple low issues remains green under the default severity-tier rule.
7. A cell with moderate and high issues is red.
8. A single critical issue is not hidden by multiple low issues.
9. Duplicate submissions do not inflate unique issue count.
10. One report with multiple detections is not automatically multiple physical issues.
11. A report with no location does not enter a spatial cell.
12. A poor-accuracy point is handled under the configured uncertainty policy.
13. Cell colour updates after severity reassessment.
14. Cell colour updates after status changes when active-only filtering is used.
15. Filter changes recompute the cells.
16. Switching modes preserves filters.
17. A cell opens its contributing issue list.
18. A cell centre is never presented as an exact issue coordinate.
19. Different cell resolutions are labelled and comparable only where appropriate.
20. Empty filters produce an honest empty state.

## 118. INTEGRATION TEST CASES

Test at least:

1. A newly submitted report with valid GPS appears after the configured lifecycle event.
2. A report appears neutral while AI analysis is pending.
3. AI analysis completes and the marker adopts the correct severity tier.
4. The heatmap uses the same severity result.
5. A duplicate is confirmed and issue counts update correctly.
6. A duplicate candidate is rejected and distinct issue points remain.
7. A status change updates both map modes.
8. A location correction updates both map modes.
9. A report becomes resolved and active-only heatmap excludes it.
10. A resolved report can still be viewed when historical status is selected.
11. A report is reopened and returns to active maps under policy.
12. AI analysis fails but the valid report location remains available.
13. Map provider failure does not delete report data.
14. Heatmap failure does not break pinpoint mode.
15. Network disconnection labels data stale.
16. Refresh does not create duplicate markers.
17. Schema version change is handled by the map adapter.
18. Legacy report without location is retained as unlocated.
19. Invalid report coordinate does not crash the map.
20. Public map output does not expose personal data.

## 119. TEST AUTOMATION

Create unit tests for coordinate validation, location-source precedence, severity-colour mapping, filter semantics, issue grouping, heatmap cell aggregation, and missing-data handling.

Create integration tests for report-to-map synchronization.

Create browser-level tests for GPS permission states, map-mode switching, marker selection, cell selection, viewport changes, and refresh behaviour where the test environment permits.

Use deterministic test coordinates and fixture records that are clearly marked as test data.

Do not use live citizen location data in automated tests.

## 120. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The map work is not complete until all of the following are true:

- The existing application remains functional.
- The map is connected to actual report data.
- The pinpoint map and severity heatmap both work.
- Both modes use the same canonical issue/report data and filter rules.
- The mode switch preserves the filter state.
- GPS capture works where supported and permitted.
- GPS-denied and desktop flows remain usable.
- Problem location and reporter/device location are not confused.
- Coordinate provenance and accuracy are retained.
- Map features link to the correct report and AI analysis.
- The default severity palette is consistent: green low, blue moderate, red high/critical, neutral grey unknown.
- Heatmap cells aggregate unique physical issues rather than raw detections.
- A critical issue cannot be hidden by averaging with lower-severity issues under the default rule.
- Unknown severity never appears as low severity.
- Duplicate records are handled using the existing physical issue identity and duplicate policy.
- Unlocated reports are not silently discarded.
- Tumakuru operating area is configured without inventing official boundaries or ward data.
- Refreshes do not create duplicated points or stale heatmap cells.
- Provider keys and location data are handled safely.
- Errors and empty states are honest.
- Tests cover GPS, map modes, heatmap, duplicate handling, filters, and AI-report integration.

