# PART 26 — END-TO-END REFERENCE WORKFLOW

Follow this conceptual workflow while preserving the existing application's actual architecture.

## Stage 1 — Report creation

A citizen creates a report and attaches an image or other supported evidence.

The report receives its persistent report identifier through the existing application.

## Stage 2 — Location acquisition

The application requests GPS only when the user initiates the relevant location action and permission is available.

The system captures the location source, coordinate, horizontal accuracy, timestamp, and permission result.

The user confirms whether the selected coordinate represents the problem location or merely the capture/observation location.

## Stage 3 — Location validation

The coordinate is checked for valid numeric ranges, accuracy, timestamp, source, and configured jurisdiction.

The raw GPS observation is preserved.

An invalid or unavailable location remains unlocated rather than being fabricated.

## Stage 4 — AI analysis

The previous UrbanPulse AI master prompt processes the image/report and returns the structured assessment.

The mapping system does not repeat object detection or alter the AI severity/priority values.

## Stage 5 — Issue identity association

Each detection is associated with a physical issue only according to the existing issue-association and duplicate policy.

Confirmed duplicate reports link to the same physical issue. Possible duplicates remain provisional.

## Stage 6 — Map adapter

The map adapter combines the report reference, physical issue ID, AI issue-level assessment, status, verification, timestamps, and validated location metadata into normalized map features.

## Stage 7 — Pinpoint map

Each independently located physical issue is displayed as a discrete marker. If only report-level GPS exists, the map displays a report-observation marker with explicit semantics.

Marker colours follow the AI severity tier.

## Stage 8 — Heatmap aggregation

Eligible unique physical issues are assigned to deterministic spatial cells at the configured resolution.

Each cell calculates unique issue count, severity distribution, category distribution, status distribution, report count, priority summary, and location-quality summary.

The cell colour follows the highest assessed issue severity under the default rule.

## Stage 9 — Shared filtering

The same filters apply to both modes. Mode switching changes representation only.

## Stage 10 — Report/map updates

New reports, AI results, duplicate decisions, location corrections, severity changes, and status changes refresh the relevant map features and heatmap aggregates.

## Stage 11 — Human review

An authorized reviewer may verify an issue or location. The original AI result and original GPS observation remain auditable.

## Stage 12 — Quality assurance

The system validates that counts, coordinates, colours, issue identities, and filters remain consistent across both map modes.

